  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and both exist in Kibana. The rule is enabled, running, and reports status `ok`. I wasn't able to check that its 5xx filter actually matches documents in the data. Kibana's console proxy is turned off in this setup, so I couldn't run a test query.\n\n**1. Fields in the data view.** The data view \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each record is one web request, with:\n- `response`: the HTTP status, stored as a string\n- `bytes`: response size\n- `clientip`: client address\n- `url` and `request`: what was asked for\n- `extension`, `agent`, `machine.os`: file type, browser, operating system\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`: source and destination country\n- `referer`, `tags`, `host`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-4eac2710 Web logs overview\".** It has 7 panels and defaults to the last 7 days: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-4eac2710-web-logs-overview-7f9e5e7a4ce5\n- Three single-number panels: total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of each response code's share of requests.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs with request count and bytes.\n- Requests by source country.\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-4eac2710 5xx spike\".** The id is `81cf5831-a8c1-4c9e-8bd0-63871a569093`.\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" in the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10.\n- I created it already switched on (Kibana's default is off) because you asked for a rule that fires. It has no actions attached, so it raises alerts in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone.",
